BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (  # The Myth of the Golden Spring) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (  In ancient times, there lived two wanderers who discovered a magical spring. The first wandere) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (    r, the Giver, possessed silver coins. The second, the Keeper, guarded the spring's basin.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (  The spring held a wondrous power: any silver dropped into its waters would transform into thre) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (    e times its worth in gold. But only the Keeper could retrieve the gold and decide how to div) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (    ide it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (  The first day, the Giver approached with doubt, offering only one coin. It became three golden) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (     coins, and the Keeper, seeing the Giver's caution, returned one. Both walked away with litt) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (    le gain.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (  The second day, the Giver remembered: *trust multiplies blessings*. He offered three coins. Th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (    ey became nine golden coins. The Keeper, recognizing the Giver's faith, returned five. Both ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 58.0 Td (    prospered.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.0 Tf 48.0 24.0 Td (Page 5 of 295) Tj ET
